  // ─── [/DAMAGE] ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  // ─── [COVERAGE] ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Does the edit SET actually achieve the instruction? Every other pass
  // judges edits one at a time, so nothing notices what was never proposed.
  // That is how "make this a three-way NDA" produced edits that missed the
  // preamble: no bucket named it, so no pass ever looked at it.
  //
  // The question is INVERTED on purpose. Asking "did I miss anything?" over
  // the same document with the same instruction reproduces the same blind
  // spot — models miss things consistently, not randomly. Instead the edits
  // are spliced into a copy and the model reads THE RESULT cold:
  //
  //     "Here is a three-way NDA. What is wrong with it?"
  //
  // A document opening "by and between A and B" fails that immediately.
  // Gaps surface as defects in a finished document rather than as omissions
  // from a plan, which is the form models are good at spotting.
  //
  // TO REMOVE: delete to [/COVERAGE] + its stage in _runPlan.
  // Keep auditing until the checklist is clean, or eight rounds. Three
  // was not enough: a run that still had gaps at round 3 simply stopped
